<section xml:id="sect-uniqueness">
  <title>Uniqueness of LOP Optimal Solutions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Throughout this text, we have generally been interested in a solution to a problem.
      However, there are times if we would like the solution to be unique or if it isn't unique, can we find other solutions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's return to one of the first example we saw in this text in <xref ref="prob-lop1"/>.
      The simplex method was developed and used to solve this in <xref ref="sect-tableau"/> and the final simplex tableau was listed in <xref ref="eq-tableau-LOP1-step4"/>, but reproduced here:
      <me>
        \left[\begin{array}{rr|rrrr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 8\\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 16\\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 40\\ \hline 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 56\\ \end{array} \right]
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The key to knowing that this is an optimal solution comes from the objective row:
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} x_4 + x_5 + z \amp = 56  \amp \amp \text{or} \amp z \amp =  56 - x_4 -x_5 \end{aligned}
      </me>
      and if either <m>x_4</m> or <m>x_5</m> is brought into the basis, then the value of the variables increases and the objective is decreased.
      From this we can conclude that the solution
      <me>
        \boldsymbol{x} = \left[ \begin{array}{rr|rrr} 8 \amp 16 \amp 40 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]
      </me>
      is optimal and unique.
    </p>

    <p>
      ADD some INFO RELATED TO THE DUAL.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Linear Optimization Problems with non-unique solutions</title>

    <p>
      Consider a seemingly minor change to <xref ref="prob-lop1"/>:
    </p>


    <problem xml:id="prob-lop1-revisit">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} \text{Maximize} \amp\amp z = 2x_1 + 4x_2,\amp \\ \text{subject to}\amp\amp 4x_1 + 3x_2 \amp \leq 120, \\ \amp \amp x_1 + 2x_2 \amp \leq 40, \\ \amp \amp x_2 \amp \leq 16, \\ \text{and} \amp \amp x_1, x_2 \amp \geq 0.
            \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </problem>

    <p>
      If we solve this using the simplex method, the final tableau is
      <me>
        \left[\begin{array}{rr|rrrr|r} 5 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 120\\ 0 \amp 5 \amp -1 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 40\\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 40\\ \hline 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 10 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 400\\ \end{array} \right]
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      which has the solution:
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} \boldsymbol{x} \amp = \frac{1}{5}\left[ \begin{array}{rr|rrr} 120 \amp 40 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 40 \end{array}\right] \\ \amp  = \left[ \begin{array}{rr|rrr} 24 \amp 8 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 8\end{array}\right] \end{aligned}
      </me>
      and <m>z= 400/5 = 80</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This is an optimal solution because writing the objective as <m>z = 400 - 10x_4</m> shows that increasing <m>x_4</m> would decrease the objective.
    </p>

    <p>
      But wait.
      Looking at the objective, it does not depend on <m>x_3</m>, the other parameter.
      So if we bring that into the basis, and removing <m>x_5</m> for the same reason we do in phase II, we get the following tableau:
    </p>

    <p>
      <me>
        \left[\begin{array}{rr|rrrr|r} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 8\\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 16\\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 40\\ \hline 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 80\\ \end{array} \right]
      </me>
      with the basic solution:
      <me>
        \boldsymbol{x} = \left[ \begin{array}{rr|rrr} 8 \amp 16 \amp  40 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]
      </me>
      and the objective is still <m>z=80</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      So it seems like we have two solutions that are optimal.
      Are there just two solutions?  Let's take a look at the feasible set again.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-lop-1-revisit">
      <caption>A plot of a feasible set of <xref ref="prob-lop1-revisit"/>
      together with level sets of the objective function <m>z=2x_1+4x_2</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.2]
            \fill[lightgray] (0,0)--(30,0) -- (24,8) -- (8,16) -- (0,16) -- cycle;
            \draw [->] (-2,0) -- (45,0) node [above right] {$x_2=0$};
            \foreach \x in {5,10,...,30} \draw (\x,3pt) -- (\x,-3pt) node [below] {\x};
            \draw [->] (0,-2) -- (0,32) node [above right] {$x_1=0$};
            \foreach \y in {5,10,...,30} \draw (3pt,\y) -- (-3pt,\y) node [left] {\y};
            \draw[<->,thick] (10, {(120-4*(10))/3}) -- (32, {(120-4*(32))/3})
              node[pos=0, left]{$x_3=0$};
            \draw[<->,thick] (-5, {(40-1*(-5))/2}) -- (45, {(40-1*(45))/2})
              node[pos=0.75, above right] {$x_4=0$};
            \draw[<->,thick] (-5,16) -- (45,16)
              node [pos = 0.6, above]{$x_5=0$};
            \foreach \s in {70,75,...,90}
              \draw[<->] (-2, {(\s - 2*(-2))/4}) -- (40, {(\s-2*(40))/4});
          \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There are 5 level sets plotted above and the middle one is when <m>z=80</m>, the optimal value found above.
      In this case, the value of the objective along the upper right edge of the feasible set is always the same.
      That is the objective value is the same for all values on the line segment between <m>(8,16)</m> and <m>(24,8)</m>.
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find other points along the line segment between <m>(8,16)</m> and <m>(24,8)</m> and show that the objective value is equal along there.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Other points on this edge include <m>(10,15)</m>, <m>(14,13)</m> and <m>(20,10)</m>.
          The value of the objective for each of these is 80.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>


